# #185: this domain is Cloudflare-proxied (DNS resolves to Cloudflare's
# anycast ranges, not the droplet's own IP — confirmed 2026-09-04), so
# every request Caddy sees arrives from a Cloudflare edge node, not the
# real visitor. Without `trusted_proxies`, Caddy's default reverse_proxy
# behavior blindly APPENDS the real connecting peer's IP onto whatever
# X-Forwarded-For a client already sent, rather than validating it first —
# so a spoofed `X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4` from anyone on the internet
# reaches backend/core/deps.py's get_rate_limit_identifier() as the
# caller's "IP", trivially defeating every IP-keyed rate limit
# (repositories/rate_limits.py backs contribution_submit, export_request_
# access, anilist_lookup — see #139/#141/#165).
#
# Listing Cloudflare's published IP ranges here (https://www.cloudflare.com/
# ips/, current as of 2026-09-04 — refresh periodically, they change rarely)
# tells Caddy which peers are legitimate upstream hops it can trust to have
# set forwarding headers honestly. For any connection whose actual TCP peer
# is NOT in this list — including someone bypassing Cloudflare entirely and
# connecting straight to the droplet's own IP with a forged
# X-Forwarded-For/CF-Connecting-IP header, since nothing else here blocks
# that — Caddy ignores whatever forwarding headers it sent and uses the
# real peer address instead. This is what actually closes the spoofing
# hole; it's a network-level check (who really connected), not a
# header-content check (which is exactly what's spoofable).
#
# `client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP` — added after a REAL bug caught live
# during post-merge verification (2026-09-04), not part of the original
# design: with the default `client_ip_headers` (X-Forwarded-For), Caddy
# resolved the "trusted client IP" to one of Cloudflare's own internal hop
# addresses (e.g. 172.71.178.125, 162.158.216.86 — both themselves inside
# the trusted ranges above) instead of the real originating visitor.
# Root cause: Cloudflare's edge network can involve more than one internal
# hop before reaching this origin, and every one of those hops' addresses
# also falls inside Cloudflare's own published ranges — so Caddy's
# right-to-left X-Forwarded-For walk (skipping entries it recognizes as
# trusted proxies) never finds a clearly "untrusted, therefore real"
# entry to stop at, and keeps walking into Cloudflare's own internal
# network as if it were the client. Confirmed directly: two requests sent
# with deliberately different spoofed X-Forwarded-For values, both
# correctly ignored (neither spoofed value ever reached
# rate_limit_events.identifier — the actual spoofing vulnerability #185
# exists for was already closed), but each request also resolved to a
# different Cloudflare-owned address rather than any real, consistent
# per-visitor IP — as bad for rate-limiting's actual purpose as the
# original bug, just a different failure mode. Cloudflare's own docs
# recommend CF-Connecting-IP specifically for this reason: their edge
# sets it exactly once, authoritatively, to the true original client IP,
# regardless of how many internal CF-to-CF hops happen afterward — no
# chain-walking needed. Still gated by the same trusted_proxies peer
# check above: a request that bypasses Cloudflare and forges this header
# directly is unaffected, since Caddy only honors it when the actual TCP
# peer is a genuine Cloudflare address.
{
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static \
 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 103.22.200.0/22 103.31.4.0/22 \
 141.101.64.0/18 108.162.192.0/18 190.93.240.0/20 188.114.96.0/20 \
 197.234.240.0/22 198.41.128.0/17 162.158.0.0/15 104.16.0.0/13 \
 104.24.0.0/14 172.64.0.0/13 131.0.72.0/22 \
 2400:cb00::/32 2606:4700::/32 2803:f800::/32 2405:b500::/32 \
 2405:8100::/32 2a06:98c0::/29 2c0f:f248::/32
		client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP
	}
}

anifillerpedia.wiki {
	# Backend-owned paths (#45): the versioned API, plus FastAPI's own
	# Swagger UI / ReDoc / schema — #198's real guide page lives at
	# /docs/guide instead of /docs specifically because this matcher claims
	# the bare /docs path for Swagger UI; /openapi.json directly rather than
	# proxying them through the frontend. Everything else (the Astro app)
	# falls through to the frontend handle.
	# #170: encode gzip is scoped to the frontend handle only, not
	# @backend — Caddy weakens a strong ETag into `W/"...-gzip"` on any
	# response it actually compresses, which breaks If-None-Match
	# round-tripping against the API's own strong ETags (#155). The two
	# API payloads this affects are small, so losing gzip there to keep
	# ETags byte-exact is the right trade. If-Modified-Since is
	# untouched either way (Last-Modified isn't affected by encoding).
	@backend path /api/v1/* /docs /redoc /openapi.json
	handle @backend {
		reverse_proxy app:8000
	}
	handle {
		encode gzip
		reverse_proxy frontend:4321

		# #186: Content-Security-Policy, scoped to the frontend handle only
		# — deliberately NOT applied to @backend above. Checked live before
		# picking that split: /docs and /redoc are FastAPI's own default
		# Swagger UI / ReDoc pages, which load their JS/CSS bundles from
		# cdn.jsdelivr.net (plus their own inline init <script>, and ReDoc
		# pulls a different Google Fonts family than the frontend's own).
		# A `default-src 'self'` CSP applied there would break both pages
		# outright — out of scope to change per #198 ("Any change to the
		# Swagger UI itself... is out of scope"), so the CSP is scoped to
		# where this app's own content actually renders instead.
		#
		# script-src: 'self' covers every same-origin bundled/external
		# script (e.g. moderation.astro's script — the one inline <script>
		# big enough that Vite externalizes it to its own hashed
		# /_astro/*.js file rather than inlining it). The rest of this
		# app's client-side <script> blocks (Header.astro's language
		# switcher, SeriesDetailPage.astro, account.astro,
		# propose-series.astro) come back from Astro's SSR build genuinely
		# INLINE in the rendered HTML rather than externalized — confirmed
		# by building the real frontend Docker image locally and diffing
		# its output against live production byte-for-byte, not assumed.
		# 'unsafe-inline' would defeat the entire point of this CSP (it's
		# meant to be defense-in-depth against #184's javascript:-href XSS
		# vector, and 'unsafe-inline' permits exactly that), so each of
		# those 4 scripts' exact SHA-256 content hash is allow-listed
		# instead. None of the 4 embed any per-request/server-interpolated
		# value (only build-time constants and client-side JS template
		# literals), so their content — and hash — stays stable across
		# requests; it only changes if a future PR edits one of these
		# specific inline <script> blocks, at which point its build will
		# fail CSP until the corresponding hash here is updated to match.
		# https://challenges.cloudflare.com is allow-listed for #20's
		# Turnstile widget loader (not live yet as of 2026-09-04 — no site
		# key provisioned — but already coded into propose-series.astro,
		# see CLAUDE.local.md's external-account checklist).
		# style-src: 'unsafe-inline' is a deliberate, accepted exception —
		# Astro inlines this app's component-scoped CSS directly as
		# literal <style> blocks (confirmed the same way as the script
		# hashes above), and unlike scripts there's no per-page-load hash
		# list that would stay maintainable here (near-every page has its
		# own scoped style block, vs. 4 known inline scripts total).
		# Inline-style injection's security impact is far lower than
		# inline-script injection — it can't execute arbitrary JS — so
		# this is a standard, widely-accepted trade for a static
		# Caddy-level CSP.
		# img-src: https://*.anilist.co for #67's synced cover/banner art
		# (confirmed live: s4.anilist.co, Cloudflare-fronted so which `s`
		# subdomain serves a given image can vary).
		# frame-src: https://challenges.cloudflare.com for Turnstile's
		# actual challenge iframe (separate from script-src, which only
		# covers loading its JS loader).
		# frame-ancestors 'none' (+ X-Frame-Options below, for older
		# clients that don't parse CSP frame directives): closes the
		# clickjacking gap #186 flagged against contribute.astro/
		# propose-series.astro/moderation/admin.
		header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com 'sha256-M6Cuk2FFQBCp22YeSZqrXki3fef9XcL2wzySAh4+J5k=' 'sha256-ZmJM6Tl01dZrC+lFQ431fOs2I92cdK0KAvAiBxOH6EU=' 'sha256-bpBxy6wc20yfG0VBfDOGNLQUEegmy+4Va9X1F4G3HgY=' 'sha256-UfKLzF4zeLAfkHOgiJCRm2ua8jNT0k8x6PEOWzkpJCw='; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: https://*.anilist.co; connect-src 'self'; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'"
	}

	# #186: baseline security headers that DON'T conflict with Swagger
	# UI/ReDoc, applied at the site level so they cover both branches above
	# (frontend HTML and the API's JSON, plus /docs and /redoc) in one
	# place — the full CSP above is the one exception, scoped narrower for
	# the reasons explained there.
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
	}

	log {
		output file /data/access.log
		format json
	}
}

# anifillerpedia.org and animefillerpedia.org redirect to the primary
# domain — registered 2026-08-21, both forward to .wiki (CLAUDE.local.md
# Live instance table). Actual redirect (registrar-level forwarding vs.
# Caddy-level) is Andreas's call during #25's provisioning; this block
# covers it here too in case Caddy-level redirect is preferred instead.
anifillerpedia.org, animefillerpedia.org {
	redir https://anifillerpedia.wiki{uri} permanent
}
